From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: grimtickle-overlay <overlay@localhost>
Date: Sat Oct 31 14:20:00 2026 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] usb: cdns3: sky1: make SuperSpeedPlus an opt-in boot parameter

SuperSpeedPlus (USB 3.2 Gen2x1, 10Gbps) has been force-disabled in
cdnsp_sky1_probe() since the 7.1 series because sustained 10Gbps I/O on
the MS-R1 CDNSP/cix-usbdp-phy stack produced periodic device resets.
That workaround is still the safe default.

Add a core boot parameter, "cix_usb_ssp_enable", defaulting to 0.  When
set, the forced disable is skipped and the firmware/XEC configuration
keeps bit 31 of XEC_CFG_3XPORT_MODE set, allowing the host controllers
to train at SuperSpeedPlus.  The ACPI "ssp-disable" property remains
authoritative: firmware that sets it still forces 5Gbps.

This provides a low-risk test switch for validating 10Gbps operation
without changing the default behaviour of any existing system.

Signed-off-by: grimtickle-overlay <overlay@localhost>
---
 drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
@@ -25,6 +25,18 @@
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.h>
+
+/*
+ * SuperSpeedPlus (USB 3.2 Gen2x1, 10Gbps) is opt-in.
+ *
+ * The vendor default (and this overlay's safe default) caps every CIX
+ * CDNSP-Sky1 SSP host port at Gen1/5Gbps because sustained 10Gbps I/O on
+ * the MS-R1's CDNSP/cix-usbdp-phy stack has historically produced periodic
+ * device resets.  Pass "cix_usb_ssp_enable=1" on the kernel command line
+ * to allow SSP for controlled testing; the default stays 5Gbps.
+ */
+static bool cix_usb_ssp_enable;
+core_param(cix_usb_ssp_enable, cix_usb_ssp_enable, bool, 0444);
 
 #include "core.h"
 #include "../host/xhci.h"
@@ -752,17 +764,16 @@
 	data->ssp_disable = device_property_read_bool(dev, "ssp-disable");
 
 	/*
-	 * Force SuperSpeedPlus (10Gbps) off unconditionally, regardless of
-	 * the "ssp-disable" ACPI property. Sustained 10Gbps I/O on the
-	 * MS-R1's cdnsp-sky1/cix-usbdp-phy stack remained unreliable
-	 * (periodic device resets under load) even after the full PHY
-	 * reset-sequencing, ACPI resource-lookup, and RTS5453 ARA hardening
-	 * done in the preceding patches. Capping at Gen1/5Gbps is a
-	 * pragmatic stability workaround, not a confirmed root-cause fix -
-	 * see the accompanying note files for suspected causes still under
-	 * investigation.
+	 * Keep the vendor's stable Gen1/5Gbps cap unless explicitly opted
+	 * out of on the command line.  "ssp-disable" from firmware still
+	 * forces the cap off (i.e. SSP disabled) even when the parameter is
+	 * set.
 	 */
-	data->ssp_disable = true;
+	if (!cix_usb_ssp_enable)
+		data->ssp_disable = true;
+	else
+		dev_warn(dev,
+			 "cix_usb_ssp_enable=1: SuperSpeedPlus enabled experimentally; 10Gbps stability is not validated\n");
 
 	if (!device_property_read_u32(dev, "sof_clk_freq", &ret))
 		data->sof_clk_freq = ret;
